1
00:00:01,330 --> 00:00:03,570
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,810 --> 00:00:04,250
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,670 --> 00:00:09,710
My name is Catherine and today we're tackling a very difficult issue for many English learners.

4
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:13,970
We're talking about taking things from other people and giving them back.

5
00:00:14,230 --> 00:00:14,710
That's right.

6
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:22,870
So today we are going to talk about money and we're going to lend money and borrow money and we're going to talk more about this after we listen to the dialogue.

7
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:31,160
Can I borrow five bucks?

8
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:31,800
No.

9
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:32,860
Come on.

10
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:34,060
I'll pay you back on Tuesday.

11
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:37,240
Last time I lent you money you never paid me back.

12
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:42,220
I promise if you lend me five dollars today I will repay you in full next week.

13
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:43,160
Okay.

14
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,380
But I'm taking your skateboard as collateral.

15
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:46,320
Fine.

16
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:48,500
I can't believe you don't trust me.

17
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:48,920
It's nothing personal.

18
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:50,160
Just business.

19
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:54,500
All right.

20
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:57,070
So now let's take a look at that language on Language Takeaway.

21
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:00,580
Language Takeaway.

22
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,670
So first up we have the key verbs for today.

23
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,620
The verb to borrow and to lend.

24
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:14,550
Now this is a really important difference to make because many people make this mistake where they use the wrong verb.

25
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:18,650
Now if I ask you Marco, can I borrow your bicycle?

26
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:24,380
That means can I take your bicycle from you and then later I'll give it back.

27
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:25,090
But I want to use it.

28
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:25,710
Right.

29
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:27,580
So the verb is kind of reflexive.

30
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:28,070
It's going on to you.

31
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:29,490
You want to borrow.

32
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:30,970
I want to borrow.

33
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:34,450
I want to take it from you for a short time and then I want to give it back.

34
00:01:34,730 --> 00:01:35,310
So to borrow.

35
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:42,550
Now the other verb is to lend and that on the other hand goes from me to you.

36
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:45,050
So you borrow my bicycle.

37
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:47,660
I lend you my bicycle.

38
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:48,880
I give it to you.

39
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:49,400
That's right.

40
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:56,860
So another way of asking can I borrow is can you lend me your bike?

41
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:58,420
That means can you act?

42
00:01:58,420 --> 00:01:59,820
Can you do this thing for me?

43
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:04,380
So there's two different ways of saying it and it's very important to remember who is doing what.

44
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:04,800
Exactly.

45
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:08,160
So remember the person taking is borrowing.

46
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,560
The person giving is lending.

47
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:18,140
So it's not correct to say can you borrow me your bicycle?

48
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:19,520
That's not right.

49
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:21,220
No.

50
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:22,220
Can you lend me?

51
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:23,600
Like can you lend me a hand?

52
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:24,520
Can you help me?

53
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Right.

54
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,000
So remember this.

55
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:27,880
To borrow is something I do.

56
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,080
I want to borrow from you.

57
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:32,700
It's the person, the subject in a sentence.

58
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:35,680
But in the case of lending, someone else is doing it.

59
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:39,280
So can you lend to me or can you lend it to him?

60
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:41,020
You have to say who it's being lent to.

61
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:41,500
Exactly.

62
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:45,080
And as you said who it's being lent to.

63
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:48,400
So the past tense of lend is lent.

64
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:49,480
Yeah.

65
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:54,040
So Marco lent me his bike last week and I broke it.

66
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:56,340
So now I have to pay him a lot of money.

67
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:00,140
You have to pay me five bucks and that's our next word, bucks.

68
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:01,580
Bucks.

69
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:03,680
Now this is a slang term.

70
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:08,220
So this is something we say very casually in America when we want to talk about dollars.

71
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:10,540
So one buck is one dollar.

72
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:13,180
A hundred bucks is a hundred dollars.

73
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:14,620
All right.

74
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:15,700
Now as you said it's very American.

75
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:20,540
You wouldn't really use it like, I don't know if you're in Britain, you wouldn't say a hundred bucks.

76
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:21,080
No, you would say quid.

77
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:22,540
Quid.

78
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:23,540
Yeah, exactly.

79
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:25,180
So they don't have dollars so they don't say bucks.

80
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:25,440
Not in England.

81
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:27,180
All right.

82
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:28,180
Very good.

83
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:36,170
So we wanted to borrow five dollars and that person promised to pay you back on Tuesday.

84
00:03:36,470 --> 00:03:38,110
I'll pay you back on Tuesday.

85
00:03:38,490 --> 00:03:38,770
All right.

86
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:40,550
So this is a lot like that verb to lend.

87
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:45,870
To lend him, to pay you, you have to say who you're going to pay, right?

88
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,170
So Marco, I'm sorry about your bicycle.

89
00:03:48,590 --> 00:03:50,030
I will pay you back tomorrow.

90
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:51,300
Okay.

91
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:54,010
That means I will give you money for it tomorrow.

92
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:58,790
So if you borrow money, you have to pay it back.

93
00:03:59,370 --> 00:03:59,710
All right.

94
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:02,060
So I want to borrow five dollars.

95
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:04,440
I promise I will pay you back Thursday.

96
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:05,660
Very good.

97
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:06,320
Okay.

98
00:04:07,420 --> 00:04:12,940
So now lastly, we will lend money, but of course we need a guarantee.

99
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:15,240
We need something to make sure that you will pay me back.

100
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:17,120
So that's called collateral.

101
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:18,100
All right.

102
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:24,870
Now collateral is important because sometimes you can't trust someone, like you say, Marco.

103
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:32,900
And so for example, if I want to rent a bicycle, so I want to use it for the day, maybe there's a store that they rent bicycles to people.

104
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:42,230
I would give my passport or my driver's license as collateral because I will definitely return to take my driver's license or passport back.

105
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:50,470
So that's basically a sign of a promise, money or identification or some object.

106
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:51,270
That's right.

107
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:57,570
So usually, for example, if you're going to get a very big loan at the bank, they will ask you for something as collateral.

108
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:01,220
So maybe you will have to give them your house as collateral.

109
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:04,320
So you're saying if I can't pay you back, then you can take my house.

110
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:05,060
That's right.

111
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:11,740
So collateral is the last word in language takeaway, but we do have some great phrases for you coming up in Fluency Builder.

112
00:05:18,990 --> 00:05:20,070
Can I borrow five bucks?

113
00:05:20,510 --> 00:05:20,690
No.

114
00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:21,750
Come on.

115
00:05:22,190 --> 00:05:22,970
I'll pay you back on Tuesday.

116
00:05:23,650 --> 00:05:26,130
Last time I lent you money, you never paid me back.

117
00:05:26,630 --> 00:05:31,130
I promise if you lend me five dollars today, I will repay you in full next week.

118
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:34,780
OK, but I'm taking your skateboard as collateral.

119
00:05:34,930 --> 00:05:35,230
Fine.

120
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:37,380
I can't believe you don't trust me.

121
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:39,050
It's nothing personal, just business.

122
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:43,360
All right, we're back.

123
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:46,100
So let's take a look at these phrases on Fluency Builder.

124
00:05:48,670 --> 00:05:49,470
Fluency Builder.

125
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:59,000
So, Marco, these are two friends talking and the way they talk to each other is very casual, so we hear at the beginning, can I borrow five bucks?

126
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:00,540
No.

127
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:01,540
Come on.

128
00:06:01,540 --> 00:06:02,540
Right.

129
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:03,540
So what is this?

130
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:04,540
Come on.

131
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:06,220
It's a persuasive phrase.

132
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:13,460
It's one of those phrases where you're trying to convince or persuade people and you can use it as an interjection in many situations.

133
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:17,300
So for example, here, come on, lend it to me.

134
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:17,800
Lend me some money.

135
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:18,620
I'll pay you back.

136
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:20,300
You're trying to convince that person.

137
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:21,300
Please.

138
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:22,300
Yeah, exactly.

139
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:23,580
But what about this situation?

140
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:25,340
I'm running late.

141
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:32,900
I have to go and my friend, she's still sitting at her desk, maybe at work and it's taking forever.

142
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:34,800
I say, come on, let's go.

143
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:35,860
Right.

144
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:36,860
It's like, hurry up, move it.

145
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:37,860
All right.

146
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:39,880
So it's a way of getting someone to do something.

147
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:41,400
You want them to follow you.

148
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:41,900
Exactly.

149
00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:48,280
And now talking about money again, the person promised to repay the money.

150
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:49,740
He said, I'll pay you back on Tuesday.

151
00:06:50,580 --> 00:06:54,940
But then he also said, I will repay you in full next week.

152
00:06:55,420 --> 00:06:56,060
In full.

153
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:57,480
Now, remember, this is important.

154
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:58,780
I will repay you in full.

155
00:06:59,180 --> 00:07:03,040
That means I won't repay you a little bit and then a little bit later.

156
00:07:03,580 --> 00:07:06,120
I will repay you everything next week.

157
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:07,020
Right, exactly.

158
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:14,900
So if you have to pay or repay someone in full, you have to give them the entire amount, not just a part of it.

159
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:15,900
That's right.

160
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:16,900
In full.

161
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:17,900
Full means everything.

162
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:20,360
The final phrase is the very last line of today's dialogue.

163
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:21,780
It's nothing personal.

164
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:23,080
It's just business.

165
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:29,720
I think this is a common phrase that people use when maybe doing business with friends or people you know.

166
00:07:30,620 --> 00:07:34,060
Maybe you're asking for collateral and the guy's like, I can't believe you don't trust me.

167
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:34,780
I'm your brother.

168
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:37,020
And he's like, it's nothing personal.

169
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:38,020
It's just business.

170
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:40,180
It's a way to say, listen, this is not about you and me.

171
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:41,380
This is about my rules.

172
00:07:41,700 --> 00:07:44,680
And when I give something to someone, I always ask for collateral.

173
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:45,980
So it's just business.

174
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:47,640
It's just my way of doing this.

175
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:49,300
Exactly.

176
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:54,260
So it's a good phrase to use if you're doing maybe business with a friend.

177
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:59,020
And of course, maybe you're setting these rules and maybe your friend feels a little bit offended.

178
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:01,160
You say, hey, it's nothing personal.

179
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:02,500
It's just business.

180
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:02,760
Nothing personal.

181
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:04,460
So that's the last phrase.

182
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:06,960
Let's take a listen to the dialogue again and we'll be right back.

183
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:15,240
Can I borrow five bucks?

184
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:15,900
No.

185
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:16,920
Come on.

186
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:18,160
I'll pay you back on Tuesday.

187
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:21,340
Last time I lent you money, you never paid me back.

188
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:26,300
I promise if you lend me five dollars today, I will repay you in full next week.

189
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:29,940
OK, but I'm taking your skateboard as collateral.

190
00:08:30,180 --> 00:08:30,400
Fine.

191
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:32,540
I can't believe you don't trust me.

192
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:33,040
It's nothing personal.

193
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:34,260
Just business.

194
00:08:34,940 --> 00:08:35,940
All right.

195
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,480
So lending money, borrowing money.

196
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:46,240
Now, as we mentioned, sometimes you have people that ask you for money.

197
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:52,300
Now, did you ever have that friend that always asked you for money and never repaid you?

198
00:08:52,300 --> 00:08:53,300
Yes.

199
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:53,540
Like a dollar at a time.

200
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:55,300
Hey, can I borrow a dollar?

201
00:08:55,300 --> 00:08:56,300
Can I borrow five dollars?

202
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:57,300
Yeah, always.

203
00:08:57,300 --> 00:09:03,920
And actually this friend, he would borrow ten dollars and he'd say, oh, I'll buy you a coffee tomorrow.

204
00:09:04,340 --> 00:09:06,240
I say, what, a ten dollar coffee?

205
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:07,240
I don't think so.

206
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:08,680
So it was always uneven.

207
00:09:09,020 --> 00:09:12,220
And I paid more and he never repaid me in full.

208
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:12,520
Right.

209
00:09:13,220 --> 00:09:14,220
So, yeah.

210
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:23,340
And actually, I think if you add up all those little that little amount of money that maybe some friends borrowed from you, it would be quite a decent amount.

211
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:28,690
I had many friends who would be like, hey, can I borrow a dollar or, you know, even like 25 cents at a time.

212
00:09:29,050 --> 00:09:30,220
It would add up over time.

213
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:30,870
After a couple of years.

214
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:33,820
Yeah, that's that's like a whole dinner.

215
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:34,820
Exactly.

216
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:37,620
So what do you call these people?

217
00:09:37,620 --> 00:09:40,700
The word in English that's a slang word is called a mooch.

218
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:41,290
A mooch.

219
00:09:41,730 --> 00:09:42,270
He's a mooch.

220
00:09:42,270 --> 00:09:49,720
It means he's always taking money from people or asking other people to pay for his dinner or staying at someone's house.

221
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:53,200
So a mooch is someone who tries to get things for free.

222
00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:54,620
Right.

223
00:09:54,620 --> 00:09:54,900
That's exactly it.

224
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:57,880
I had friends like this that would for food as well.

225
00:09:58,140 --> 00:09:59,240
Like maybe I brought my lunch.

226
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:00,820
This is in school, obviously.

227
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:04,340
And they would mooch off me like half my sandwich or half my juice.

228
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:05,340
Can I have some chips?

229
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:05,620
Can I have some juice?

230
00:10:06,340 --> 00:10:07,340
Yeah, exactly.

231
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:08,340
So that's the mooch behavior.

232
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:10,260
So that's the verb to mooch off someone.

233
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:11,360
Yeah, to mooch off someone.

234
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:12,100
But it's also a noun.

235
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:13,260
He's a mooch.

236
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:13,920
He's a mooch.

237
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:15,320
Very interesting stuff.

238
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:19,260
Maybe you have a friend that was a mooch or maybe even yourself back in the day.

239
00:10:19,460 --> 00:10:22,260
So let us know how it works in your country.

240
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:25,500
If you lend money to people, family members, do you ask for collateral?

241
00:10:25,970 --> 00:10:27,850
Or do you just forget about it?

242
